<article class="env-example" id="0FG6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FG6">Example <span data-tag="0FG6">45.3.7</span>.</a> Let $X = \mathbf{P}^1_ k$. Then we have </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{Corr}^0(X, X) = \mathop{\mathrm{CH}}\nolimits ^1(X \times X) \otimes \mathbf{Q} = \mathop{\mathrm{CH}}\nolimits _1(X \times X) \otimes \mathbf{Q}  \]
</div>
  <p> Choose a $k$-rational point $x \in X$ and consider the cycles $c_0 = [x \times X]$ and $c_2 = [X \times x]$. A computation shows that $1 = [\Delta ] = c_0 + c_2$ in $\text{Corr}^0(X, X)$ and that we have the following rules for composition $c_0 \circ c_0 = c_0$, $c_0 \circ c_2 = 0$, $c_2 \circ c_0 = 0$, and $c_2 \circ c_2 = c_2$. In other words, $c_0$ and $c_2$ are orthogonal idempotents in the algebra $\text{Corr}^0(X, X)$ and in fact we get </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{Corr}^0(X, X) = \mathbf{Q} \times \mathbf{Q}  \]
</div>
  <p> as a $\mathbf{Q}$-algebra. </p>
</article>